Subject: [PATCH] d_cosim: evaluate the co-simulation at the operating point

A d_cosim block reported all-zero outputs at t=0 and for the whole of the
first timestep, where an equivalent NgVeri (Verilator) model of the same
Verilog is already correct.  Any design whose correct initial output is 1 --
an inverter, a NAND with both inputs low, anything held by a reset -- was
wrong at the start of every simulation, so the two co-simulation backends
disagreed on identical sources.

Measured on eSim's three-gate reference circuit (AND + OR + NAND, driven
through an adc_bridge, read back through a dac_bridge), against the eSim 2.5
NgVeri run of the same schematic:

    before:  nand output 0 V from t=0 until 2.506 us, then correct
             "WARNING: output scheduled with impossible delay (-2.504e-06)"
    after:   nand output 5 V at t=0, no warning
             logic identical to the 2.5 reference over the full 25 ms
             every transition within 0.000 ns of the reference

Two things were wrong, one on each side of the d_cosim/ivlng boundary.

1. src/xspice/verilog/vpi.c -- next_advance_cb()

   SPICE asks for time zero at the operating point.  That gives ticks == 0,
   and the loop went straight back to waiting for a later time, so VVP never
   executed its time-zero events and never reported the design's initial
   outputs.  Settle once, using the zero-length set_stop() that output_cb()
   already uses to gather everything happening at the current instant: a
   zero-length request registers cbReadWriteSynch instead of cbAfterDelay, so
   VVP runs the pending events without the clock moving.  Guarded by a new
   ng_vvp.zero_settled so it happens exactly once and the two time bases stay
   aligned.

2. src/xspice/icm/digital/d_cosim/cfunc.mod -- ucm_d_cosim()

   The TIME == 0.0 branch pushed inputs into the co-simulator and returned
   without running it, leaving every output at the ZERO written during INIT.
   The co-simulation was first stepped to time zero down in STEP_PENDING,
   which does not run until the first accepted timestep -- and that also left
   vtime lagging SPICE time by a whole step, which is what produced the
   "impossible delay" warning.  Step at the operating point instead and
   publish the result through a new op_output(), which writes the port state
   directly rather than scheduling a delayed event, because the operating
   point has no future: the value is the DC state of the node.  The
   "first step" test in STEP_PENDING now keys on a new instance.zero_done so
   it fires exactly once wherever time zero was reached first, and still
   works as the fallback for an analysis that never calls the model at
   TIME == 0.

Clocked behaviour is unchanged: on a divide-by-2 flip-flop regression the
toggle edges land at identical times before and after, and the patch removes
a spurious 1.5 ns startup glitch on the inverted output.
---
diff -Naur a/src/xspice/icm/digital/d_cosim/cfunc.mod b/src/xspice/icm/digital/d_cosim/cfunc.mod
--- a/src/xspice/icm/digital/d_cosim/cfunc.mod	2025-08-31 19:52:14.000000000 +0530
+++ b/src/xspice/icm/digital/d_cosim/cfunc.mod	2026-07-29 15:50:41.138622100 +0530
@@ -78,6 +78,7 @@
     unsigned int    out_ports;   // Number of XSPICE outputs.
     unsigned int    inout_ports; // Number of XSPICE inout ports.
     unsigned int    op_pending;  // Output is pending.
+    unsigned int    zero_done;   // Co-simulation has been stepped to time 0.
     Digital_t      *out_vals;    // The new output values.
     double          last_step;   // Time of previous accepted step.
     double          extra;       // Margin to extend timestep.
@@ -272,6 +273,69 @@
     ip->op_pending = 0;
 }
 
+/* Publish co-simulator output at the operating point (eSim addition).
+ *
+ * output() above cannot be used here.  It schedules each change as an event
+ * PARAM(delay) after the difference between SPICE time and the co-simulator's
+ * time, but the operating point has no elapsed time and no future: the value
+ * IS the DC state of the node.  So write the port state directly, exactly as
+ * the INIT branch of ucm_d_cosim() establishes the initial ZERO, and keep the
+ * rotating "currently published" storage in step so that the first real
+ * output() during the transient does not re-emit a change already made here.
+ *
+ * OUTPUT_CHANGED is still set, because that is what makes ngspice re-run the
+ * event solver at the operating point and let the new digital state propagate
+ * (to a following dac_bridge, for instance).  It is the same mechanism that
+ * the "Operating point event passes" statistic counts.
+ */
+
+static void op_output(struct instance *ip, ARGS)
+{
+    Digital_t    *out_vals; // XSPICE rotating memory
+    unsigned int  i, j;
+
+    if (!ip->op_pending) {
+        for (i = 0; i < ip->out_ports; ++i)
+            OUTPUT_CHANGED(d_out[i]) = FALSE;
+        for (i = 0; i < ip->inout_ports; ++i)
+            OUTPUT_CHANGED(d_inout[i]) = FALSE;
+        return;
+    }
+
+    out_vals = (Digital_t *)cm_event_get_ptr(1, 0);
+
+    for (i = 0; i < ip->out_ports; ++i) {
+        if (ip->out_vals[i].state != out_vals[i].state ||
+            ip->out_vals[i].strength != out_vals[i].strength) {
+            DBG("OP: OUT %s %d/%d->%d/%d",
+                cm_get_node_name("d_out", i),
+                out_vals[i].state, out_vals[i].strength,
+                ip->out_vals[i].state, ip->out_vals[i].strength);
+            out_vals[i] = ip->out_vals[i];
+            OUTPUT_STATE(d_out[i]) = out_vals[i].state;
+            OUTPUT_STRENGTH(d_out[i]) = out_vals[i].strength;
+            OUTPUT_DELAY(d_out[i]) = PARAM(delay);
+            OUTPUT_CHANGED(d_out[i]) = TRUE;
+        } else {
+            OUTPUT_CHANGED(d_out[i]) = FALSE;
+        }
+    }
+
+    for (i = 0, j = ip->out_ports; i < ip->inout_ports; ++i, ++j) {
+        if (ip->out_vals[j].state != out_vals[j].state ||
+            ip->out_vals[j].strength != out_vals[j].strength) {
+            out_vals[j] = ip->out_vals[j];
+            OUTPUT_STATE(d_inout[i]) = out_vals[j].state;
+            OUTPUT_STRENGTH(d_inout[i]) = out_vals[j].strength;
+            OUTPUT_DELAY(d_inout[i]) = PARAM(delay);
+            OUTPUT_CHANGED(d_inout[i]) = TRUE;
+        } else {
+            OUTPUT_CHANGED(d_inout[i]) = FALSE;
+        }
+    }
+    ip->op_pending = 0;
+}
+
 /* Run the co-simulation. Return 1 if the timestep was truncated. */
 
 static int advance(struct instance *ip, ARGS)
@@ -630,7 +694,33 @@
     in_vals = (Digital_t *)cm_event_get_ptr(0, 0);
 
     if (TIME == 0.0) {
-        /* Starting, so inputs may be settling. */
+        /* The operating point.  Inputs may still be settling, so ngspice can
+         * call in here more than once ("Operating point event passes" in the
+         * event statistics).
+         *
+         * eSim change: this branch used to push the inputs into the
+         * co-simulator and return WITHOUT running it, leaving every output at
+         * the ZERO that the INIT branch above wrote.  The co-simulation was
+         * first stepped to time zero down in the STEP_PENDING branch, which
+         * does not run until the first accepted timestep.  Consequences:
+         *
+         *   - Any design whose correct output at t=0 is 1 -- an inverter, a
+         *     NAND with both inputs low, anything held by a reset -- reported
+         *     0 through the operating point and the whole of the first
+         *     timestep.  A compiled NgVeri/Verilator model computes its
+         *     outputs on this same call and is right at t=0, so the two
+         *     backends disagreed on identical Verilog.
+         *   - The co-simulator's vtime stayed at 0 while SPICE time reached
+         *     the first timestep, so the first output() computed a negative
+         *     delay and warned "output scheduled with impossible delay".
+         *
+         * Stepping here is free and safe: Cosim_setup() has already started
+         * the client simulator and it is parked at time zero.  Running it
+         * with vtime still 0 only lets it settle its time-zero events, which
+         * is precisely the digital operating point.  A repeat pass re-pushes
+         * the settled inputs and settles again; the client never leaves time
+         * zero, so nothing is replayed and nothing is rewound.
+         */
 
         for (i = 0; i < ip->in_ports; ++i) {
             Digital_t ival;
@@ -640,17 +730,20 @@
             in_vals[i] = ival;
         }
 
-        for (i = 0; i < ip->out_ports; ++i)
-            OUTPUT_CHANGED(d_out[i]) = FALSE;
-
         for (i = 0; i < ip->inout_ports; ++i) {
             Digital_t ival;
 
             ival = *(Digital_t *)INPUT(d_inout[i]);
             (*ip->info.in_fn)(&ip->info, i + ip->in_ports, &ival);
             in_vals[i + ip->in_ports] = ival;
-            OUTPUT_CHANGED(d_inout[i]) = FALSE;
         }
+
+        /* Settle the co-simulation at time zero and publish what it says. */
+
+        ip->info.vtime = 0.0;
+        (*ip->info.step)(&ip->info);
+        ip->zero_done = 1;
+        op_output(ip, XSPICE_ARG);
         return;
     }
 
@@ -715,11 +808,21 @@
          * forward, replaying any saved input events.
          */
 
-        if (ip->last_step == 0.0) {
+        if (!ip->zero_done) {
             /* First step.  "Step" the co-simulation to time zero,
              * as that may trigger initialisations.
+             *
+             * eSim change: the test was "ip->last_step == 0.0", which is also
+             * true after the operating point has already stepped to time zero
+             * (last_step is only set once a step is accepted).  Keying on
+             * zero_done instead means this runs exactly once, wherever time
+             * zero was reached first.  It is still the fallback for an
+             * analysis that never calls the model at TIME == 0, so it must
+             * set the flag itself: unlike last_step, which run() updates on
+             * every accepted step, nothing else clears this one.
              */
 
+            ip->zero_done = 1;
             if (advance(ip, XSPICE_ARG))
                 return;
         }
diff -Naur a/src/xspice/verilog/icarus_shim.h b/src/xspice/verilog/icarus_shim.h
--- a/src/xspice/verilog/icarus_shim.h	2025-08-31 19:52:14.000000000 +0530
+++ b/src/xspice/verilog/icarus_shim.h	2026-07-29 15:50:41.184653100 +0530
@@ -59,6 +59,7 @@
 struct ng_vvp {
     struct cr_ctx       cr_ctx;         // Coroutine context.
     int                 stop;           // Indicates simulation is over.
+    int                 zero_settled;   // Time-zero events have been run.
     struct co_info     *cosim_context;
     uint32_t            ins;            // Port counts by type.
     uint32_t            outs;
diff -Naur a/src/xspice/verilog/vpi.c b/src/xspice/verilog/vpi.c
--- a/src/xspice/verilog/vpi.c	2025-08-31 19:52:14.000000000 +0530
+++ b/src/xspice/verilog/vpi.c	2026-07-29 15:50:41.229726000 +0530
@@ -165,6 +165,33 @@
             ctx->stop_cb = set_stop(ticks, ctx);
             return 0;
         }
+
+        /* eSim change: settle the design once at time zero.
+         *
+         * SPICE asks for time zero at the operating point, which gives
+         * ticks == 0, and the loop above then went straight back to waiting
+         * for a later time -- so VVP never executed its time-zero events and
+         * the design's initial outputs were never reported.  d_cosim was left
+         * publishing the ZERO it writes at INIT, so any design whose correct
+         * initial output is 1 read as 0 until the first timestep, unlike an
+         * equivalent Verilator model.
+         *
+         * set_stop(0) is the mechanism already used by output_cb() below to
+         * gather everything happening at the current instant: a zero-length
+         * request registers cbReadWriteSynch rather than cbAfterDelay, so VVP
+         * runs the pending events at the current time and calls back without
+         * the clock moving.  Doing it once, guarded, keeps the two time bases
+         * aligned: VVP stays at time zero, and the next entry here finds
+         * zero_settled set and parks as before.
+         */
+
+        if (!ctx->zero_settled) {
+            DBG("Settling at time zero (VVP %g, SPICE %g)\n",
+                vl_time, ctx->cosim_context->vtime);
+            ctx->zero_settled = 1;
+            ctx->stop_cb = set_stop(0, ctx);
+            return 0;
+        }
     }
 }
 
